    ],
    "justification": "The equality identifies both the universal Airy law and the contrast-specific response-alphabet curvature in the unrestricted multi-arm game.",
    "gap": "The closest sharp theorem is the two-arm expansion of Sudijono, Dobriban, and Tchetgen Tchetgen (2026); no searched source gives a multi-arm response-alphabet coefficient or an all-face lower bound.",
    "consumer": "For an ACTG-175-style four-regimen binary trial, the theorem determines whether the prespecified regimen-versus-average allocation and nonlinear shrinkage rule are second-order minimax.",
    "proof_tex": "On the component with at least three active arms, the upper theorem gives\n\\[\n\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}n^{4/3}d_n(c)\\ge\\Lambda_c,\n\\]\nwhereas the lower theorem gives the reverse upper limit.  The two limits coincide, so\n\\[\nR_n(c)=n^{-1}-\\Lambda_cn^{-4/3}+o(n^{-4/3})\n=n^{-1}-C_AV_c(s_c/2)^{2/3}n^{-4/3}+o(n^{-4/3}).\n\\]\nThe proof of the upper theorem uses exactly the product design with \\(q_a^\\star=|c_a|/2\\) and the total full-label nonlinear estimator \\(\\delta_n^\\star\\), so that pair attains the expansion.\n\nFor \\(K=2\\), the two-arm reduction proposition proves \\(q^\\star=(1/2,1/2)\\) and \\(V_c(s_c/2)=1\\), while its cited published component gives the unrestricted two-arm risk expansion.  Interchanging the two arm labels and negating the reported estimator preserves squared risk, so the same statement holds for both \\((1,-1)\\) and \\((-1,1)\\).  These are exactly the two components of the union in the theorem, and no claim is made for a contrast outside that union.",
